### Discussed in https://github.com/bevyengine/bevy/discussions/4008

<div type='discussions-op-text'>

<sup>Originally posted by **vringar** February 21, 2022</sup>
Hey,

I'm trying to build a UI that doesn't use Val::px anywhere to ensure that the UI will scale seamlessly for smaller displays, however I'm running into an issue, when trying to get text wrapping to work.

This is the almost minimal code example I could come up with to show the issue:

```rust
use bevy::prelude::*;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .insert_resource(ClearColor(Color::rgb(0.4, 0.4, 0.4)))
        .insert_resource(WindowDescriptor {
            title: "TextDemo".to_string(),
            vsync: true,
            ..Default::default()
        })
        .insert_resource(Msaa { samples: 4 })
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_startup_system(setup)
        .run();
}

fn setup(mut commands: Commands, asset_server: Res<AssetServer>){
    commands.spawn_bundle(UiCameraBundle::default());

    commands
        .spawn_bundle(NodeBundle {
            // Root Panel, covers the entire screen
            style: Style {
                size: Size::new(Val::Percent(100.0), Val::Percent(100.0)),
                justify_content: JustifyContent::SpaceBetween,
                ..Default::default()
            },
            color: Color::NONE.into(),
            ..Default::default()
        })
        .with_children(|parent| {
            spawn_left_panel(parent, &asset_server);
        });

}

fn spawn_left_panel(parent: &mut ChildBuilder, asset_server: &Res<AssetServer>) {
    parent
        .spawn_bundle(NodeBundle {
            style: Style {
                flex_direction: FlexDirection::ColumnReverse,
                size: Size::new(Val::Percent(20.0), Val::Percent(100.0)),
                ..Default::default()
            },
            color: Color::rgb(0.10, 0.10, 0.10).into(),
            ..Default::default()
        })
        .with_children(|parent| {
            parent
                .spawn_bundle(TextBundle {
                    text: Text::with_section(
                        "This is a super long text, that I would hope would get wrapped. Unfortunately it just breaks out of the box and keeps going",
                        TextStyle {
                            font: asset_server.load("FiraSans-Bold.ttf"),
                            font_size: 20.0,
                            color: Color::rgb(1.0, 1.0, 1.0),
                        },
                        Default::default(),
                    ),
                    style: Style {
                        position: Rect {
                            top: Val::Percent(1.0),
                            left: Val::Percent(2.0),
                            ..Default::default()
                        },
                        max_size: Size::new(Val::Percent(100.0), Val::Undefined),
                        ..Default::default()
                    },
                    ..Default::default()
                });
        });
}
```

This results in the following display:
<img width="954" alt="image" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13276717/155017869-cc2ff226-398a-4276-a5e2-377328e85cd3.png">

I would have assumed that ` max_size: Size::new(Val::Percent(100.0), Val::Undefined)` would mean that the box can't get bigger than its parent. But that is not how it works. Is the error in my mental model, my code or bevy?
</div>

## Bevy version

0.8

## Relevant system information

Win10 / Vulkan

```ignore
AdapterInfo { name: "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070", vendor: 4318, device: 7938, device_type: DiscreteGpu, backend: Vulkan }
```

## What you did

To work around #1490, constrain the size of a text in an attempt to wrap it:

```rust
                .spawn_bundle(NodeBundle {
                    style: Style {
                        flex_direction: FlexDirection::ColumnReverse, // top to bottom
                        align_items: AlignItems::FlexStart,           // align content top
                        ..default()
                    },
                    color: UiColor(Color::rgba_u8(122, 47, 125, 214)),
                    ..default()
                })
                .with_children(|parent| {
                    parent
                        .spawn_bundle(TextBundle {
                            text: Text::from_section(
                                "",
                                TextStyle {
                                    font: font.clone(),
                                    font_size: 17.0,
                                    color: Color::GRAY,
                                },
                            ),
                            style: Style {
                                size: Size::new(Val::Px(490.), Val::Auto), // #1490 - Text wrapping should work with a percent width
                                ..default()
                            },
                            ..default()
                        });
                });
```

## What went wrong

Without the size, or with a percentage (#1490):

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6532395/187161536-e1ba82bf-d66d-4302-9037-ccd7bdc91505.png)

With the code above:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6532395/187161677-35c156cf-09ad-4959-bc04-32f9cbb332ea.png)

But now with a shorter text and the same size constraint, some top padding appeared:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6532395/187161820-648233e1-08e4-4f37-8ecc-a907d994b14a.png)

And it's proportional to the constraint width, here's for `370.` instead of `490.`:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6532395/187161942-25575c43-6677-49cf-91f9-3d2ff97c1dc1.png)

When the constraint reaches the actual calculated text size, the padding disappear. When going further below, the text as expected starts to wrap again.

## Additional information

The text vertical alignment is ignored, so cannot even work around by vertically aligning to top. I'm not sure with the size is counted toward the text wrapping, since the size is that of the node, whereas text wrapping should work with the actual calculated size of the glyphs.

body:
The workaround is to split text into the words and let Taffy layout it, you can achieve even `text-aligment: justify` or any rich text effect with this approach:

![text_aligment](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/122736/197634240-cf597385-c4e6-4f3c-8f6f-1e82ead997fa.gif)

I've dive a bit into code (thank you @djeedai for pointing), and it looks difficult to fix this the issue without rewriting a bit of code. Taffy is pretty good at layouting (except for maybe #5834), but it is best when it knows atomic element sizes (the size of each word for example). It calculates all layout into a single pass and I believe it is better to keep it as it is. It could be an idea to rework TextBundle to let Taffy layout text as well: as far as I understend, ui already batches text nodes and there is shouldn't be lot of performance lost. Custom plugin would do the job as well.

There is a gist (https://gist.github.com/jkb0o/58ee5980ef6077b39f91979f7f19cddc), project archieve ([bevy_example_text_aligment.zip](https://github.com/bevyengine/bevy/files/9855616/bevy_example_text_aligment.zip)) and inline code:
```rust
//! This example illustrates how you can cheat text layout with using bevy (with taffy)
//! It works with latest bevy from the main branch (at the date of writing)
//! ref 6aa2dce
//! https://github.com/bevyengine/bevy/tree/6aa2dce
//! 
//! Resize the window to see the effect

use bevy::prelude::*;

const FONT_SIZE: f32 = 25.0;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_startup_system(setup)
        .run();
}

fn setup(mut commands: Commands, asset_server: Res<AssetServer>) {
    commands.spawn(Camera2dBundle::default());

    // I will split this text into the words and let Taffy to
    // layout it with FlexWrap::Wrap
    let text = "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet consectetur adipiscing elit. Maecenas auctor, nunc ac faucibus fringilla."
        .to_string();

    commands.spawn(NodeBundle {
        style: Style {
            size: Size::new(Val::Percent(50.), Val::Undefined),
            // you can achieve text-align properties using justify content:
            // - JustifyContent::Center for `center`
            // - JustifyContent::FlexStart for `left`
            // - JustifyContent::FlexEnd for `right`
            // - JustifyContent::SpaceBetween for `justify` 
            //   (it looks better when add extra space at the end of the string.
            //   even better if you add empty node with `flex_grow: 1`)

            justify_content: JustifyContent::SpaceBetween,
            flex_wrap: FlexWrap::Wrap,
            ..default()
        },
        ..default()
    }).with_children(|commands| {
        for word in text.split(" ") {
            commands.spawn((
                TextBundle::from_section(
                    word.to_string(),
                    TextStyle {
                        font: asset_server.load("FiraMono-Medium.ttf"),
                        font_size: FONT_SIZE,
                        color: Color::WHITE,
                    },
                )
                .with_text_alignment(TextAlignment::TOP_CENTER)
                .with_style(Style {
                    // this is required becouse of the bevy bug
                    // https://github.com/bevyengine/bevy/issues/5834
                    max_size: Size::new(Val::Undefined, Val::Px(FONT_SIZE)),

                    // this is the size of the spaces between words
                    margin: UiRect::all(Val::Px(3.)),
                    ..default()
                }),
            ));
        }
        // comment it for everithing except `justify` text aligment
        commands.spawn(NodeBundle {
            style: Style {
                flex_grow: 1.,
                ..default()
            },
            ..default()
        });
    });
}
```

Ok, I finally got back to it.
This worked on my first try. And I guess in a similar way I would spawn new children (new text) for the entity (text container). I have to say tho, this feels a little weird. To make such mutating operations on `Entity`, that for some reason is in the Query without either `&` or `&mut`. Just that seams to me unusual in bevy (maybe I don't undestand something properly). And also the `&Children` get despawned, even though they are just borrowed.

Also in here https://bevy-cheatbook.github.io/features/parent-child.html#known-pitfalls I found that I also should call the despawning separately from the removing. I hope that is still the right way how to do it.

```rust
fn despawn_text (
    mut commands: Commands,
    text_box: Query<(Entity, &Children), With<TextBox>>
) {
    let (entity, children) = text_box.single();
    commands.entity(entity).remove_children(children);
    for child in children {
        commands.entity(*child).despawn_recursive();
    }
}
```
Please let me know if you have some clarifying comments on this.
But all in all I can work with this and thank you very much for the help :)

